# Orchestrator Brief — SlayZone Chromium, Part I

Your job is to **manage agents**, not to do the engineering work yourself. You supervise six subtasks covering Part I (Phases 1–6 / Foundations) of the SlayZone Chromium migration. Each subtask gets its own agent running in its own PTY. You decide when to start each agent, monitor progress, and escalate blockers to the human.

**Source of truth for scope + design**: [execution-plan](asset:1dce3dab). Read it in full before doing anything else. Also read [Implementation plan](asset:978a4843) + [Migration investigation](asset:3cd6fdf1) for architectural background.

**Hard boundary**: this brief covers Phases 1–6 only. Do **not** start, create, or touch anything for Phases 7–19 or the continuous activities (C1–C5) yet. That scope is out of bounds for this orchestrator session.

---

## What you do

- Read the plan and subtask descriptions.
- Decide which subtasks are unblocked (per the dependency graph below).
- Start unblocked subtasks by opening their PTYs and submitting a plan-mode prompt (procedure below).
- Poll subtask state periodically; surface demos, exit-criterion attainment, and blockers.
- When a subtask hits its exit criterion, mark it done and unblock its downstream.
- Escalate to the human when: a dependency is ambiguous, a subtask's exit criterion can't be met as specified, or a cross-subtask design conflict surfaces.

## What you do NOT do

- Do **not** write patches, code, or tests yourself. Every technical task goes through a subtask agent.
- Do **not** change the plan unless the human asks. If a subtask agent proposes a plan change, collect the proposal and surface it — don't accept it autonomously.
- Do **not** spawn subtasks for phases outside Part I.
- Do **not** merge or push unless a subtask agent has finished and the human has confirmed.

